## Key findings (regenerated from the model)

- **The design point works, but carries no contingency.** <<plan_dry>> t dry batches (about <<plan_kiln_m3>> m³ kilns) at <<plan_bpd>> batches per kiln-day, with feedstock dried to ≤<<plan_moisture>> moisture, deliver <<plan_det>> t deterministically. Across <<mc_runs>> Monte Carlo runs the P50 is <<plan_p50>> t (P10–P90 <<plan_p10>>–<<plan_p90>>, P(≥100k) <<plan_prob>>). On published Kon-Tiki values the network delivers <<lit_net>> t, so kiln throughput is the first thing to measure.
- **Recovering rejected tonnes is about <<lev_ratio>>× cheaper than producing new ones.** A dMRV QA desk recovers tonnes at <<lev1_cost>>/t. Capacity levers (more kilns, a second shift, more operating days) cost about <<lev2_cost>>/t, close to full unit cost (<<cost_t>>/t; breakeven <<breakeven>> against a <<price>> anchor). The top three levers close <<lev_top3_gap>> of the gap between the P50 and the target.
- **Late payments to villages show up later as lost supply.** In the synthetic year, the site with the worst payment record (<<worst_sla>> paid within <<sla_days>> days, against <<median_sla>> elsewhere) lost about half its supply <<onset_gap>> weeks after payments broke SLA. Paying on time is a delivery control, and it is the integrity record buyers and rating agencies look at.

